{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-51433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/c9373fff819b3c54ac7aa776642019ac7d4023656aa586d70b3899f821fcfd2c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-51433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=-2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is not a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=3,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-51433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88