ction does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$   the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-114280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$"] ["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114280" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=1$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/8b81ef12b7193ecbaf0b9b3c12b5a7c99fd72b4562c4197700062bf654822a8c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=-1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at  $x=1,$  the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=-1$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "categor