pture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-51429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-51429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-51429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=0$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=1$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=4$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-51429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=1$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/8f20b09e85ce71a1001e7c701ebfafa3e6ac6c8adb29e8dfd47f256f37a29f5f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,1.5)$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114399" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-114399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114399" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/