hange concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-79200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3"] ["math" "x=-4"] ["math" "x=0"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-3$ and $x=4$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+x$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-16\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-16\\cdot (-1)>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-16\\cdot (1)<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8},$ we conclude that, in ascending order, \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n are indeed points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-344059" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344059" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344059" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344059" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=27{x}^{2}+5x-4+\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =54x+5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{6}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =54+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{3}}=52>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-\\frac{1}{6}) & =54+\\frac{2}{{(-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}}=-378<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we conclude that $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344064" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-344064" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344064" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-2x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1$  and $x=1.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =\\frac{2(1-(-2)^{2})}{(1+(-2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2(1-(0)^{2})}{(1+(0)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2(1-(2)^{2})}{(1+(2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are indeed points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-114409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-1"] ["math" "x=1"] ["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=0$"] ["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=1$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-1$ and $x=1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-314762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-314762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{6}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\,(2-3{x}^{2})\\,{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}(2-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we ignored the negative square root since $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}\\,(2-3\\,{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}){e}^{-1/(1/2)^{2}} \\\\  & =128\\,(2-\\frac{3}{4}){e}^{-4} \\\\  & =160{e}^{-4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\,(2-3)\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}},$ we conclude that $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-314762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-314762" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-314762" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344246" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-16x+26) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-10x+10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-114407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=2$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+30x-42).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+30x-42)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-18x+30) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-12) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-12)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-12x+12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=4,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2})=-4{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-3(1)^{2})=-2e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4)^{3}-3(4)^{2})=16{e}^{4}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=3$ is an inflection point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-344242" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344242" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-6x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+48x-6 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}+60x+48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}+60x+48 & =0 \\\\ 12(x+4)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-5,\\,$ $x=-2,\\,$ and $x=0,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-5) & =12(-5)^{2}+60(-5)+48=48>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}+60(-2)+48=-24<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}+60(0)+48=48>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-344226" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344226" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344226" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-344226" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =5{x}^{4}+20{x}^{3} \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =20{x}^{3}+60{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{x}^{3}+60{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & =20(-4)^{3}+60(-4)^{2}=-320<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & =20(-1)^{3}+60(-1)^{2}=40>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & =20(1)^{3}+60(1)^{2}=80>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=-3$ is a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6a63de4c5e2bc9e8346c877ebf97d88a81300f9bef1d35d05a6cc202be70fd30"} {"question" "q-79208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3"] ["math" "x=0"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=0$"] ["text" "$x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=3$"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "q-79208" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/state.json" "file_sha256" "d6b4b197424b83a96f51c510aee83ade4007f9068e9bd11ea0df23ab2a04da19"} {"question" "e-3739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3739.json" "file_sha256" "f818220a017ac07ad065640089031451ccb552b29aa585cbdec09cd4fad2ccf9"} {"question" "e-3739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3739.json" "file_sha256" "f818220a017ac07ad065640089031451ccb552b29aa585cbdec09cd4fad2ccf9"} {"question" "e-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3742.json" "file_sha256" "2d815d0e011baaaf3e8388700a4b2cdfeb16d116809efad81bdd769de5f52d70"} {"question" "e-3742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3742.json" "file_sha256" "2d815d0e011baaaf3e8388700a4b2cdfeb16d116809efad81bdd769de5f52d70"} {"question" "e-3746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3746.json" "file_sha256" "8ba4e7ba4649991a28c1656c41d794ccc95c97bcb65ffe479e193179cb80ecdf"} {"question" "e-3746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3746.json" "file_sha256" "8ba4e7ba4649991a28c1656c41d794ccc95c97bcb65ffe479e193179cb80ecdf"} {"question" "e-3747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3747.json" "file_sha256" "56849833c0042561d39745f6b1815fde431c655249cf9cc9a698d0a5d4badf2f"} {"question" "e-3747" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/example-3747.json" "file_sha256" "56849833c0042561d39745f6b1815fde431c655249cf9cc9a698d0a5d4badf2f"}] "usage" {"q-114407" [] "q-51428" [] "e-3747" [] "e-3742" [] "e-3746" [] "e-3739" []} "basis" 1232 "retractions" [[17592186097238 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/q-51428.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y = f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?"] [17592186096892 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x) = e^{x}(x^{3} - 8x^{2} + 26x - 36)$."] [17592186096346 :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8)$. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/8c0a30c74a9851f457e1a17295449e84.png)"] [17592186096346 :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0$, andthe tangent line exists at this point.\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2$. Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4$, andthe tangent line exists at this point.\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6$, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only."] [17592186096347 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/b6aea7e849502bdf3bd10c401a134e62.png)"] [17592186096347 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x$.\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0$."] [17592186096348 :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22$."] [17592186096348 :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0$. However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22 \\\\ f'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\\ f''(x) & = 6x + 18 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6x+ 18 &= 0 \\\\ 6x &= -18 \\\\ x &= -3. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$. So we pick two points around it, say, $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(-4) &= 6(-4) + 18 =-6 < 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(-2) &= 6(-2) + 18 = 6 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3$, we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection."] [17592186096349 :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2$."] [17592186096349 :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0$. However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{4} - 4x^{3} + 6x^{2} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 4x^{3} - 12x^{2} + 12x \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 12x^{2} - 24x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x^{2} - 24x + 12 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1$. So we pick two points around it, say,\n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2$,\n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(0) &= 12(0)^{2} - 24(0) + 12 = 12 > 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(2) &= 12(2)^{2} - 24(2) + 12 = 12 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1$, we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186093760 17592186093761 17592186093762 17592186093763 17592186093764 17592186095786 17592186095787 17592186095788 17592186095789 17592186095790] "immutable_fields" [[17592186093759 :answer-field/correct] [17592186093759 :answer-field/id] [17592186093759 :answer-field/key] [17592186093759 :answer-field/type] [17592186095785 :answer-field/correct] [17592186095785 :answer-field/id] [17592186095785 :answer-field/key] [17592186095785 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
